    "evidence_text": "The proposed model outperforms a word-level baseline at predicting user-annotated hashtags associated with the posts, doing significantly better when the input contains many out-of-vocabulary words or unusual character sequences.\n\nIn\n\nTraditional Neural Network Language Models (NNLMs) treat words as the basic units of language and assign independent vectors to each word type.\n\nRecently,  challenge this assumption and present a bidirectional Long Short Term Memory (LSTM)  for composing word vectors from their constituent characters which can memorize the arbitrary aspects of word orthography as well as generalize to rare and out-of-vocabulary words.\n\nCompared to a word-level baseline this model shows improved performance at predicting hashtags for a held-out set of posts.\n\nRelated Work\tUsing neural networks to learn distributed representations of words dates back to  .\n\nWord Level Baseline\tSince our objective is to compare character-based and word-based approaches, we have also implemented a simple word-level encoder for tweets.\n\nA more sophisticated tokenizer may be used, but for a fair comparison we wanted to keep language specific preprocessing to a minimum.\n\nResults\tWe test the character and word-level variants by predicting hashtags for a held-out test set of posts.\n\nFinally, table 1 shows some predictions from the word level model and tweet2vec.",
